## 7\. User Flow

### 7.1 Onboarding Flow (New Organization)

1. User visits AEGIS GRC PWA URL on their mobile browser; splash screen appears for 2 seconds
2. User is redirected to `/login`; taps "Create a new workspace"
3. On `/register` Step 1, user enters Full Name, Work Email, Password, and accepts Terms — taps "Continue"
4. On Step 2, user enters Organization Name, selects Industry and Country, optionally uploads a logo — taps "Create Workspace"
5. Supabase Auth creates user account; workspace record is created in the database; user is assigned Administrator role
6. User lands on `/dashboard` — a welcome banner guides them: "Start by activating a compliance framework"
7. User taps "Add Framework" shortcut on the dashboard or navigates to `/frameworks` via bottom nav
8. On `/frameworks/add`, user browses available frameworks, taps "Activate" on ISO/IEC 27001:2022
9. Confirmation modal appears — user taps "Confirm"
10. User is navigated to `/frameworks/iso27001/controls` — all controls pre-populated from the framework template, all statuses set to "Not Started"

### 7.2 Compliance Framework Review Flow

11. On the controls list, user sees controls grouped by domain (e.g., A.5 Organizational Controls)
12. User taps on a control row (e.g., A.5.1 — Policies for Information Security)
13. On the control detail screen, user reads the description on the "Description" tab
14. User taps "Ask AI About This Control" — AI assistant panel slides up with the control pre-loaded as context
15. AI explains the control in plain language and suggests what evidence is typically expected
16. User dismisses the AI panel, taps the "Implementation Guidance" tab for structured steps
17. User taps the Status dropdown and changes it from "Not Started" to "In Progress"
18. User taps the Owner field and selects a team member from the user picker — taps "Save"
19. User taps "Create Task" — task creation modal opens pre-linked to this control
20. User fills in task title, assigns it to the selected control owner, sets a due date, sets priority to High — taps "Save Task"
21. The assigned team member receives an in-app notification and (if enabled) an email notification about the new task

### 7.3 Evidence Upload Flow

22. Control owner receives task notification, navigates to `/tasks`, opens the task on the "My Tasks" tab
23. Control owner reads task description, taps the linked control chip to view the control detail
24. On the "Evidence" tab of the control detail, control owner taps "Upload Evidence"
25. The evidence upload bottom sheet slides up; user taps the upload area and selects a PDF file from their device
26. User enters a title, description, and sets an expiry date; the linked control is pre-populated
27. User taps "Upload" — upload progress bar runs; success confirmation appears
28. The evidence item appears in the "Evidence" tab of the control with a "Valid" green badge
29. The control owner returns to the task, leaves a comment: "Evidence uploaded — access policy document attached" — taps "Post"
30. Task status is changed to "Pending Review" — compliance analyst receives an approval request notification

### 7.4 Approval and Remediation Flow

31. Compliance analyst navigates to `/tasks`, taps the "Pending Approval" tab
32. Analyst opens the task, reviews the comment, taps the linked evidence to preview the uploaded document
33. Analyst is satisfied — taps "Approve" with optional comment "Approved — evidence meets requirement"
34. Task status changes to "Approved"; control status is updated to "Implemented" by the analyst
35. Dashboard compliance percentage for ISO/IEC 27001 increments accordingly

### 7.5 Risk Management Flow

36. Compliance analyst navigates to `/risks` via bottom nav
37. Analyst taps "Add Risk" — risk creation screen opens at `/risks/new`
38. Analyst fills in risk title: "Unauthorized access to sensitive HR data", selects Category "Technical", sets Likelihood to 3 and Impact to 5 (Inherent Score auto-calculates to 15 — Critical)
39. Analyst fills in control measures, sets Residual Score to 8, assigns a risk owner, sets status to "Open", sets a due date
40. Analyst links this risk to control A.8.3 and taps "Save Risk"
41. Risk appears in the risk register list with a Critical red badge; in Matrix View, the risk dot appears in the red zone
42. Risk owner receives a notification; a related task can be created from the risk detail screen

### 7.6 AI Assistant Standalone Flow

43. User taps the "AI Assistant" bottom nav tab — navigates to `/ai-assistant`
44. User sees the disclaimer banner and a set of suggested prompt chips
45. User taps "Identify gaps in my current posture" — AI receives workspace compliance context (framework, control statuses) and responds with a structured gap analysis
46. AI identifies 3 controls with no evidence and 2 controls with expired evidence, suggests creating tasks for each
47. User taps "Export as Task" on one of the AI's suggestions — task creation modal opens with AI suggestion pre-filled as the task description
48. User confirms task details and assigns to the relevant owner — taps "Save Task"

### 7.7 Audit Readiness and Reporting Flow

49. As audit date approaches, Compliance Analyst navigates to `/reports` via side drawer
50. The Audit Readiness Score card shows 68% for ISO/IEC 27001 — amber status
51. Analyst selects "Evidence Coverage" report type — chart shows 32 controls without linked evidence
52. Analyst exports the report as PDF to share with management — taps "Export PDF", download initiates
53. Analyst switches to "Control Status Summary" report, reviews by domain, identifies A.9 (Access Control) domain as lagging
54. Analyst navigates back to the frameworks controls list, filters by A.9 domain and "Not Started" status, performs bulk assignment of an owner to all unstarted A.9 controls
55. Analyst sets up a scheduled weekly compliance health report: taps "Schedule Report", selects "Overall Compliance Health", frequency "Weekly", adds management email addresses — taps "Save Schedule"
56. Auditor (external) is invited via `/settings` → Team tab → "Invite Member" with Auditor role
57. Auditor accepts invitation email, logs in, and navigates to `/audit-log` — sees a read-only chronological log of all compliance activities, status changes, and evidence uploads
58. Auditor can navigate to individual framework controls and view linked evidence in read-only mode, providing audit confidence in the compliance record

### 7.8 PWA Installation Flow

59. On first visit via mobile browser (Chrome/Safari), browser displays a PWA install banner or an in-app prompt appears after 30 seconds of usage
60. User taps "Add to Home Screen" — browser native install dialog appears
61. User confirms — AEGIS GRC icon appears on the device home screen
62. On subsequent launches via the home screen icon, the app opens in standalone mode (no browser chrome), with offline-cached content available immediately
63. When offline, the cached dashboard, control list, and task list are displayed; any actions taken (status updates, task completions) are queued and synced automatically when connectivity is restored

